# Karpathy Principles

## 1. Think Before Coding

Don't assume. Don't hide confusion. Surface tradeoffs.

- State assumptions explicitly before acting on them.
- Two plausible readings of the request? Present both, ask, then build.
- Name the tradeoff you chose and what it costs.
- Unknown API, schema, or convention? Read it. Never invent a signature.

## 2. Simplicity First

Minimum code that solves the problem. Nothing speculative.

- No unrequested features.
- No abstraction with a single caller.
- No config option with one possible value.
- No handling for errors that cannot occur.
- YAGNI is the default; complexity needs a present, concrete justification.

## 3. Surgical Changes

Touch only what you must. Clean up only your own mess.

- Edit the narrowest region that fixes the problem.
- Match surrounding style, even if you'd write it differently.
- Don't refactor working code you merely read.
- Unrelated bugs, dead code, formatting drift → report, don't fix in passing.

## 4. Goal-Driven Execution

Define success criteria. Loop until verified.

- Restate the task as a checkable statement before starting.
- Run the real test/lint/build command; read the actual output.
- Failed? Fix and re-run. Report only what you observed.
- "Should work" is not done.

